) (- (- 0 q) 1)))))]\n    [py/int-mod\n     ;; the remainder with the divisor's sign, from the exact |a| mod |b|\n     (fn [a b]\n       (let [r (get (py/divmod-pos (py/abs a) (py/abs b)) 1)]\n         (if (= r 0)\n           0\n1361:| Float semantics, IEEE, NaN, `-0.0`           | 1, with a tag          | All three hosts are binary64, but JS cannot distinguish `2` from `2.0`, so   |\n2067:  raises `OverflowError` and NaN raises `ValueError`.\n2089:- Infinities get their own signed keys. Every NaN shares one key,\n2090:  `[:py.numeric/nan]` (pinned in C3-S2). A float here has no object\n2091:  identity to tell two NaNs apart, and Jing float64 content already makes\n2092:  every NaN one value, so a per-object NaN key cannot survive addressing;\n2094:  `=` answers true for one boxed NaN and false for two). This departs from\n2095:  CPython, where distinct NaN objects are distinct keys. Jing's private\n2130:transport. NaN key identity and full float rendering parity remain\n2252:  preservation, NaN normalization, and the refusals of equal-value,\n2295:through `data/float-value`, pinned one NaN key (section 8.5.4), and\n2362:derivation record, asserted unchanged on Node and Dart; NaN repr, the\n2363:one quiet NaN in canonical bytes and a NaN-computing program; and lift,\n2366:integral floats, both zeros, NaN and both infinities byte for byte.\n3620:   shares content identity while retaining distinct provenance.\n35:                  {::integer/max-bits 100000, ::integer/max-digits 4300}))})\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
